<p class="lead">Klein has some of the most established neighborhoods in the Spring area. Many homes sit on lots with mature trees, settled landscaping and, quite often, a landscape lighting system that was installed years ago and has not been touched since. Spring Landscape Lighting works with Klein homeowners on both sides of that story: designing new systems for homes that have never been lit, and bringing tired halogen installations back to life with modern LED.</p>

<h2>Mature trees, settled landscapes</h2>
<p>Time has been kind to many Klein properties. Live oaks, pines and magnolias planted decades ago now frame houses and arch over driveways. These trees are the best lighting opportunity on the lot. A couple of well-placed uplights on a large oak, or moonlighting fixtures mounted high in the canopy, can do more for the property at night than a dozen lights along the foundation. See how we approach this on our <a href="/services/tree-garden-lighting/">tree and garden lighting</a> page.</p>
<p>Mature landscapes also bring challenges. Root zones are extensive, beds are dense, and older irrigation lines run everywhere. Installation needs care: hand digging near roots, routing wire around existing systems, and placing fixtures where established shrubs will not swallow them.</p>

<h2>Older halogen systems ripe for an upgrade</h2>
<p>If your home has landscape lighting that came with the house, there is a good chance it uses halogen lamps. Typical signs include yellowish light, fixtures that are noticeably dimmer than others, frequent bulb replacements and heavy, hot lamps. Halogen lamps commonly draw 20 to 50 watts each, while comparable LED fixtures usually draw 2 to 12 watts.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Older system issue</th><th>Typical fix</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Dim fixtures at the end of runs</td><td>LED conversion reduces load and voltage drop</td></tr>
<tr><td>Corroded fixtures and sockets</td><td>Replace with brass or copper LED fixtures</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mechanical timer always wrong</td><td>Astronomical timer or smart controls</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lights hidden by overgrown shrubs</td><td>Relocate and re-aim, or redesign the layout</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>An upgrade is also a chance to fix design problems: glare from old floodlights, lights aimed into windows, or a runway of path lights along the front walk. Our <a href="/services/landscape-lighting-repair/">repair and upgrade service</a> covers everything from targeted fixes to a full redesign, and adding <a href="/services/smart-lighting-controls/">smart lighting controls</a> at the same time is often worthwhile.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Tip:</strong> Curious what an LED conversion would save? Plug in your fixture count and wattage in our <a href="/tools/led-savings-calculator/">halogen to LED savings calculator</a>.</div>

<p>Spring Landscape Lighting can evaluate an existing Klein system in a single visit and tell you what is worth keeping.</p>

